  "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe model-selection and compaction neighborhood retained dead production surface and duplicated target-assembly logic after PR #122422 unified tiered model resolution.\n\nThe dead paths were `buildConfiguredAllowlistKeys` (only test callers since the fallback allowlist split in c0e4caae558), the `resolveModelAsync` option `retryTransientProviderRuntimeMiss` (its startup-prewarming caller was replaced by prepared runtime snapshots across f85cfc8b6c7 through 06f5f73e473), ignored `useAsyncModelResolution` plumbing (async resolution became unconditional; d79537d6519 removed most opt-ins but missed two), and dead facade re-exports. Separately, `resolveEmbeddedCompactionTarget` rebuilt the same result object with the auth-profile-drop rule inlined at five sites.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis is a maintainer-requested cleanup sweep. Per the Repair Doctrine, it deletes dead branches instead of preserving them, and consolidates compaction target assembly so the auth-profile-drop-on-provider-change invariant has one owner.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNone intended; this is behavior-preserving except for deleting dead paths. The plugin-SDK-shipped `prepareSimpleCompletionModelForAgent` keeps `useAsyncModelResolution` as a documented deprecated no-op field for source compatibility.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Net −93 production LOC / −106 test LOC (+83/−282 total).\n- Focused suites passed twice: the worker run covered 7 files and 469 tests; an independent rerun covered 3 shards including `model-selection`, `compaction-runtime-context`, `model-resolution-consistency`, `compact.hooks`, `simple-completion-runtime`, `tts-core`, and `model.test`.\n- `pnpm plugin-sdk:surface:check` passed.\n- Codex autoreview (`gpt-5.6-sol`, high) was clean: \"patch is correct (0.98)\".\n- New table-driven tests pin compaction override precedence for unique-provider inference, ambiguous-literal fallback, and multi-segment ID plus profile-suffix preservation.\n- Exploration NO-GO: compaction override precedence intentionally diverges from canonical alias-first resolution (PR #90885 / issue #90340), so it was consolidated rather than rewritten.\n",
